Yes. From an Elastic SME perspective, you can add some useful points that are slightly different from the Azure/OpenAI cost discussion. The strongest angle is to focus on Elastic integration, operational value, governance, and how you will measure whether the AI Assistant is actually helping.

I would add points like these:

Start with bounded Elastic use cases. Initially use AI for alert explanation, ES|QL/query assistance, investigation guidance, log/error summarisation, incident correlation, and knowledge-base retrieval. These are easier to control and measure than open-ended conversational use.

Separate “AI convenience” from “operational investigation.” Simple questions such as “what does this alert mean?” may consume relatively little, while problem-management queries covering large time windows, multiple hosts, logs and alerts could drive substantially higher token consumption.

Measure usage by use case. From the beginning, capture which teams are using AI Assistant, what types of queries they are running, query frequency, input/output token consumption and estimated cost. Otherwise it will be difficult to determine whether the £100k allocation is delivering value.

Define success criteria before scaling. For example: reduction in MTTR, faster triage, fewer manual ES|QL queries, improved first-time incident diagnosis, or reduced SME involvement. Token consumption by itself does not tell us whether the service is valuable.

Consider Elastic context size. A key cost/architecture consideration is not only the selected GPT model but how much Elastic context is passed into it. Sending large log samples, alert histories or investigation context can materially increase input tokens.

Use a model abstraction where possible. Since the Azure OpenAI deployment can apparently be changed later, avoid designing the Elastic implementation around behaviour unique to one GPT model. That makes it easier to move from GPT-5.1/mini to another approved model based on cost and performance.

Security and data governance need explicit confirmation. Before enabling production use, confirm exactly what Elasticsearch data can be included in prompts, whether sensitive fields need masking/redaction, how RBAC applies to AI Assistant context, and what prompt/response auditing is retained.

Knowledge base is a strong use case. Elastic AI Assistant becomes much more useful if it can use approved operational documentation such as runbooks, troubleshooting procedures, known errors, previous RCA guidance and application support documentation, rather than only interpreting raw telemetry.

A good point for you to raise verbally would be:

“From the Elastic side, I think we should also distinguish between simple assistant usage and deeper incident/problem-management investigations. The latter could pass considerably more Elastic context into the model, so I would like us to capture token usage and cost by use case from day one. We should also define a few measurable outcomes such as reduced triage time or MTTR, so when we review the spend we can demonstrate what operational value we are actually getting from it.”

